  NEW: <h2>
  NEW: The hours
  NEW: </h2>
  NEW: <p>
  NEW: Added on 25 November, from a third working note.
  NEW: Beside each glyph the dial gives an hour of the day or of the night.
  NEW: Carman and Evans (2014) list 22 that can be read (their Table 11) and set four aside as outliers, which leaves 18.
  NEW: I set those 18 hours against the true times of full and new moon for each of the 3,711 months that could be the dial's first, with the longitude of the place left free at each, and scored each start by how far the hours fall from the real times (root mean square).
  NEW: </p>
  NEW: <figure>
  NEW: <img src="img/hours-sixteen.png" alt="Sixteen dots, one for each start the months allow, plotted by year against how far the 18 engraved hours are from real full and new moons.
  NEW: Four dots, for 223, 205, 187 and 169 BC, lie between 2.5 and 2.7 hours, with best longitudes of 141 degrees east, 26 degrees east, 89 degrees west and 156 degrees east.
  NEW: The other twelve lie between 4.5 and 6.6 hours, about a dashed line at 5.6 hours for a start taken at random.">
  NEW: <figcaption>
  NEW: The sixteen starts that the marked months allow, tried against the hours.
  NEW: </figcaption>
  NEW: </figure>
