  NEW: </div>
  NEW: <p>
  NEW: The four are consecutive eighteen-year cycles, and the hours cannot tell them apart.
  NEW: The longitude moves 115° west with each cycle, because the cycle is about a third of a day longer than a whole number of days.
  NEW: Carman and Evans call this the "rule of 8 h", and the mechanism carries it:
  NEW: a small dial, the Exeligmos, gives 8 and 16 hours to add in the second and third cycles.
  NEW: With those added, 187 BC fits at 31° E and 169 BC at 36° E, as the second and third cycles of a dial whose first is 205 BC.
  NEW: So what the place fixes is the cycle the hours were written for.
  NEW: Carman and Evans give a longitude of best fit of 25.3° E with a root-mean-square discrepancy of 2.52 h, and conclude that the dial fits 205 BC "with the exeligmos dial set at 0".
  NEW: </p>
  NEW: <p>
  NEW: Nearly all of this is in their paper (sections 13 to 22).
  NEW: What is mine is the longitude left free at every start, every month tried as a start, and one count:
  NEW: taken alone, with the marked months left out, the hours do not fix the start, since 54 of the 3,711 starts fit them better than 205 BC does.
  NEW: Two cautions from the note.
  NEW: The start of 259 BC, which the dial note leaves out of the sixteen by one solar month, fits the hours at 10° E (3.20 h).
  NEW: And the longitude is good only to about nine degrees.
  NEW: </p>
  NEW: <p>
  NEW: The other date that has been proposed, December 178 BC, I tried the same way.
  NEW: The engraved hours are no nearer the real full and new moons that follow it than they are after a month taken at random:
  NEW: 5.4 to 5.8 h for the four months about that date.
  NEW: Its authors hold that the hours are the mechanism's own predictions and not times taken from the sky, and that account is not tested here.
==== replace
  OLD: The published datings turn on the hours engraved beside the glyphs, which I did not test.
  NEW: The published datings turn on the hours engraved beside the glyphs, which are taken up under "The hours" below.
==== insert
  NEW: Two of the eight expectations for the hours failed.
  NEW: I gave 45% to 205 BC being the best of the sixteen starts on the hours; 187 BC is, by three hundredths of an hour, which means nothing.
  NEW: And I gave 70% to 205 BC being among the best thirty of all 3,711 starts on the hours alone; 54 starts fit them better.
  NEW: </li>
  NEW: <li>
  NEW: For the hours I sealed my expectations after reading Carman and Evans's table, one of their sections and their conclusions, and not the sections in which they make the comparison I was about to make.
  NEW: The first reader of that note found credit to them missing from four of my six points.
  NEW: I had also taken one hour from a summary list that does not say day or night.
  NEW: </li>
  NEW: <li>
